{#- KNEE STRENGTH TOOL, v1 — English sibling of /members/rodillas/. Same 4
    routines, same library structure; only the copy changes.

    Original note follows.

    KNEE STRENGTH TOOL, v1 — built Aug 13, 2026 from Iván's `knee pain
    artifact.md`. Home doc: `knee-strength-brief.md`.

    Two questions (where × foam roller) → one of 4 routines. Same setup-then-
    build pattern as the activation matrix, but on the NEW strength engine
    (sets × reps + rest timer), because this is a strength prescription and a
    40-second countdown would tell someone to keep going when the prescription
    is eight good reps.

    Structure, same reason as the matrix: one exercise LIBRARY, routines are
    lists of ids. Warm-up and mobility are shared by all four; only the strength
    block and the foam-rolling block change.

    Three additions to Iván's source doc, chosen by him: the isometric wall sit
    (the one thing here that helps an athlete who hurts today), single-leg
    balance, and gym adductor work. Two more were offered and NOT taken —
    eccentric calf/soleus work and knee-flexion hamstring work — do not re-add
    them without asking; the omission is a decision, not an oversight. -#}
{% raw %}
<div class="setup" id="setup">
  <div class="crumb-bar"><div class="wrap"><a href="/members/en/#biblioteca">Library</a> / Knees</div></div>

  <div class="setup-head">
    <span class="label">Strength</span>
    <h1>Pain-free knees</h1>
    <p>Knee pain in runners and cyclists almost never starts at the knee. It starts higher up — glute medius, quads, hip — and the knee is where the bill lands. This trains the right place.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-warn">
    <strong>Before you start.</strong> This is preventive strength work, not treatment. <strong>If you have swelling, if the knee locks or gives way, if pain wakes you at night, or if it started after an impact or a twist, this is not what you need</strong> — you need someone to look at it. Write to me and we'll sort it out.
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Where are you training?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="place">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="home" aria-pressed="true">At home<span>no equipment</span></button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="gym" aria-pressed="false">At the gym<span>with weights</span></button>
    </div>
    <p class="setup-hint">Start at home even if you have a gym. The loaded version only adds something once the movement pattern is clean — loading a knee that caves inward just drives it further in.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="setup-q">
    <div class="setup-label">Do you have a foam roller?</div>
    <div class="setup-opts" data-axis="roller">
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="yes" aria-pressed="true">Yes</button>
      <button type="button" class="setup-opt" data-val="no" aria-pressed="false">No</button>
    </div>
    <p class="setup-hint">Without a roller the routine is still complete: the rolling block drops out and the stretches stay, which is the part that gives you the most range back.</p>
  </div>

  <button type="button" class="btn-primary setup-go" id="setupGo">Build my routine →</button>
</div>

<div id="toolWrap" hidden>
{% endraw %}
  {% include "partials/strength-tool.njk" %}
{% raw %}
</div>

<script>
window.KNEE_ROUTINE = (function () {

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- LIBRARY */
  const L = {
    /* --- entrada en calor (las cuatro rutinas) --- */
    swingFB: { name: "Leg Swings (Front to Back)", sets: 1, reps: "15 per side", tag: "Wall for support",
      cue: "Hold a wall and swing the leg forward and back. <strong>Build the range gradually</strong> — starting at end range is how a hamstring goes before you even start." },
    swingLat: { name: "Lateral Leg Swings", sets: 1, reps: "15 per side", tag: "Wall for support",
      cue: "Face the wall and swing the leg side to side across the body. You're opening the adductors and waking up the glute med." },
    sideRaise: { name: "Side-Lying Hip Abduction", sets: 1, reps: "20 per side", tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Lie on your side, top leg straight, toes pointed slightly toward the floor. Lift slowly. <strong>It should burn on the side of the glute</strong>: if you feel it in the groin, you're rotating the leg." },
    bridge: { name: "Glute Bridge", sets: 2, reps: "15", rest: 30, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Feet close to your glutes. Drive through the heels to lift the hips and <strong>pause two seconds at the top, squeezing the glutes</strong>. The pause is the exercise; without it these are just reps. Lower with control and don't arch the low back. If you feel it in the hamstrings, slide the feet closer." },
    balance: { name: "Single-Leg Balance", sets: 1, reps: "30s per side", hold: 30, holdSides: 2, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Stand on one leg, knee softly bent. <strong>Don't let the hip drop on the free side.</strong> Let the foot do the work: toes active, ankle making the corrections. If that's easy, close your eyes. Keep a wall or a chair within reach." },

    /* --- fuerza en casa --- */
    wallSit: { name: "Wall Sit", sets: 3, reps: "45s", hold: 45, rest: 60, tag: "Wall",
      cue: "Back to the wall, knees around 90°. Drive the heels as if dragging them backward and hold. If the knee is cranky today, start here." },
    bulgarian: { name: "Bulgarian Split Squat", sets: 3, reps: "8-12 per leg", rest: 90, tag: "Chair or couch",
      cue: "Back foot on a chair, chest tall. Front knee tracks the second toe, never inward. If it caves, shorten the range before you shorten the standard." },
    slBridge: { name: "Single-Leg Glute Bridge", sets: 3, reps: "10 per leg", rest: 60, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "One heel close to the glutes, other knee hugged to the chest. Drive through the heel and squeeze the glute at the top. <strong>If you feel it in the low back, tuck the pelvis and lower the height</strong>: the point is to isolate the glute, not let the psoas do the work again." },
    revLunge: { name: "Reverse Lunge to Knee Drive", sets: 3, reps: "12 per leg", rest: 60, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Step back into a lunge and, without pausing, drive that knee to the chest in one move. Half the exercise is not wobbling on the way up. Front knee tracks the second toe." },
    copenhagen: { name: "Modified Copenhagen Plank", sets: 3, reps: "20-30s per side", hold: 20, holdMax: 30, holdSides: 2, rest: 45, tag: "Chair or couch",
      cue: "Side plank with the top knee on a chair and the bottom leg off the floor. Body in a line. This loads the adductors." },
    stepDown: { name: "Eccentric Step-Down", sets: 3, reps: "10 per leg", rest: 60, tag: "Step",
      cue: "Stand on a step, lower the free foot toward the floor in 3–4 seconds and come up quickly. The slow lower is the whole exercise. Knee over the second toe." },

    /* --- fuerza en gimnasio --- */
    wallSitLoaded: { name: "Wall Sit (Optional Plate)", sets: 3, reps: "45s", hold: 45, rest: 60, tag: "Wall · plate optional",
      cue: "Back to the wall, knees around 90°. Drive the heels as if dragging them backward. A plate on the thighs is optional. If the knee is cranky today, start here." },
    bulgarianW: { name: "Dumbbell Bulgarian Split Squat", sets: 3, reps: "8-10 per leg", rest: 90, tag: "Dumbbells",
      cue: "Back foot elevated, dumbbells at the sides, chest tall. Front knee over the second toe. Add load only when the reps happen without the knee caving." },
    rdl: { name: "Romanian Deadlift", sets: 3, reps: "10-12", rest: 90, tag: "Barbell or dumbbells",
      cue: "Barbell or dumbbells. Hip hinge, not a squat: hips go back, knees barely move. Long spine." },
    legPress: { name: "Single-Leg Leg Press", sets: 3, reps: "10 per leg", rest: 90, tag: "Leg press",
      cue: "One foot on the platform, high and slightly turned out, so glute and VMO take the load. Control the lower; don't bounce at the bottom. Knee tracks over the second toe." },
    cableAbd: { name: "Cable Hip Abduction", sets: 3, reps: "12-15 per side", rest: 45, tag: "Low cable",
      cue: "Ankle strap on the low cable, side-on to the stack. Sweep the leg out. Torso stays still: if you lean the other way, drop the weight." },
    cableAdd: { name: "Cable or Machine Hip Adduction", sets: 3, reps: "12-15 per side", rest: 45, tag: "Cable or machine",
      cue: "Side-on to the cable, or on the adductor machine. Bring the leg in through a full range, no bounce. Don't lean the torso to help." },
    legExt: { name: "Terminal Knee Extension", sets: 3, reps: "15", rest: 45, tag: "Machine",
      cue: "On the machine, last 30° only: slightly bent to straight, moderate load, squeeze at the top. Full range shears; this sliver isolates VMO." },

    /* --- rodillo --- */
    rollQuad: { name: "Foam Roll: Quads", sets: 1, reps: "60-90s per side", hold: 60, holdMax: 90, holdSides: 2, tag: "Foam roller",
      cue: "Lie face down with the thigh on the roller, from the hip crease to just above the kneecap. Medium pressure: if you're holding your breath, it's too much. <strong>When you find a tender spot, stay there and bend and straighten the knee.</strong>" },
    rollTFL: { name: "Foam Roll: TFL", sets: 1, reps: "60-90s per side", hold: 60, holdMax: 90, holdSides: 2, tag: "Foam roller",
      cue: "The muscle high and forward on the hip, where the IT band starts. Don't roll the band on the side of the thigh: that's tendon, not muscle. Roll up here." },
    rollCalf: { name: "Foam Roll: Calf Belly", sets: 1, reps: "60-90s per side", hold: 60, holdMax: 90, holdSides: 2, tag: "Foam roller",
      cue: "<strong>Stay on the meaty part of the calf. Never roll the tendon</strong> — it is highly innervated tissue and friction there only makes it worse. Sit with the calf on the roller and the other leg crossed on top for load. When you find the spot that complains most, park there instead of sawing back and forth." },

    /* --- movilidad (las cuatro rutinas) --- */
    couch: { name: "Couch Stretch", sets: 1, reps: "2 min per side", hold: 120, holdSides: 2, tag: "Wall",
      cue: "Kneel with your back to a wall or the foot of a bed, back instep resting up, other foot forward in a lunge. <strong>Tuck the pelvis and squeeze the rear glute before you lift the torso.</strong> Skip that and the pelvis dumps, so the low back takes the stretch instead of the psoas and quad. Almost nobody needs to sit up as tall as they think." },
    pigeon: { name: "Pigeon Pose", sets: 1, reps: "2 min per side", hold: 120, holdSides: 2, tag: "No equipment",
      cue: "Front shin across, back leg long and in line — not kicked out to the side. Hinge from the hip and breathe. Don't wrestle it. If the knee complains, pad the hip." }
  };

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------- ROUTINES */
  const WARMUP = ["swingFB", "swingLat", "sideRaise", "bridge", "balance"];
  const STRENGTH = {
    home: ["wallSit", "bulgarian", "slBridge", "revLunge", "copenhagen", "stepDown"],
    gym: ["wallSitLoaded", "bulgarianW", "rdl", "legPress", "cableAbd", "cableAdd", "legExt"]
  };
  const ROLL = ["rollQuad", "rollTFL", "rollCalf"];
  const MOBILITY = ["couch", "pigeon"];

  const WHY = "When the glute medius is weak, the pelvis drops at every footfall and the knee collapses inward. That tightens the IT band and punishes the patellar tendon. So this trains glute medius to stabilise the pelvis, VMO to keep the kneecap tracking straight, and posterior chain to offset quad dominance. The knee is not the problem: it is where the bill lands.";

  const PLACE = { home: "At home · no equipment", gym: "At the gym" };

  function build(place, roller) {
    const pick = (ids) => ids.map((id) => {
      const e = L[id];
      if (!e) throw new Error("Unknown exercise id: " + id);
      return Object.assign({}, e);
    });
    const phases = [
      { name: "Warm-up", exercises: pick(WARMUP) },
      { name: "Strength", exercises: pick(STRENGTH[place]) }
    ];
    if (roller === "yes") phases.push({ name: "Rolling", exercises: pick(ROLL) });
    phases.push({ name: "Mobility", exercises: pick(MOBILITY) });

    return {
      title: "Pain-free knees",
      crumb: "Knees",
      kicker: "Strength",
      subtitle: "Warm-up, strength and mobility. Finish each set and tap the button — the only clock here is the rest between sets.",
      context: PLACE[place] + (roller === "yes" ? " · with roller" : ""),
      why: WHY,
      warning: "<strong>Twice a week</strong>, on easy endurance days. Never the day before a quality session or a long one. <br><br>Discomfort up to <strong>3 out of 10</strong> during the exercises is fine, and it has to be the same or better the next day. Past that, or worse the next morning, cut the range or the load — don't push through it.",
      doneSub: "Done. This one pays out in months, not sessions.",
      phases: phases
    };
  }

  return { build: build, library: L };
})();

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- SETUP UI */
(function () {
  const choice = { place: "home", roller: "yes" };

  document.querySelectorAll(".setup-opts").forEach((group) => {
    const axis = group.dataset.axis;
    group.querySelectorAll(".setup-opt").forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
        choice[axis] = btn.dataset.val;
        group.querySelectorAll(".setup-opt").forEach((b) =>
          b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b === btn)));
      });
    });
  });

  document.getElementById("setupGo").addEventListener("click", () => {
    window.STRENGTH_DATA = window.KNEE_ROUTINE.build(choice.place, choice.roller);
    document.getElementById("setup").hidden = true;
    document.getElementById("toolWrap").hidden = false;

    const s = document.createElement("script");
    // Fingerprinted engine URL, published by the partial as TP_ENGINE_SRC.
    // A literal path here would be unfingerprinted and therefore pinned in
    // the visitor's browser for a year by Caddy's immutable header on
    // /assets/js/* — the September 8, 2026 defect. Do not inline it back.
    s.src = window.TP_ENGINE_SRC;
    document.body.appendChild(s);

    // Reload rather than re-initialise: the engine holds its rest timer in
    // closure with no teardown, and a stale interval firing under a second
    // routine is a worse bug than a page load. Same call as the matrix.
    s.addEventListener("load", () => {
      const back = document.createElement("button");
      back.className = "change-link setup-back";
      back.textContent = "← Change options";
      back.addEventListener("click", () => location.reload());
      document.querySelector(".tool").prepend(back);
    });
  });
})();
</script>
{% endraw %}
